# The quiet layer: the slate's edge over the world, and the two apps that read
# the land. Every frame here is reachable from a normal game start.
#   tools/tour.sh tours/slate-hud.tour --seed=1 --hour=19 --weather=clear:0 --explore=6000
coast calm

# --- The location ping sits on its own scrap of glass --------------------------
# Walking into a landscape names it. Over the snowfield at noon the name used to
# be phosphor green on near-white paper at 3.5:1, with the ping's own ring
# running through the letters; it is read off a plate now, at 16:1, and the ring
# goes out around it.
place snowfield
await ping 8
# A third of the way out, which is where the ring is worth a picture: the plate
# is already opaque, the lettering has barely started, and the ring is clear of
# the plate's rim with most of its ink still to spend.
wait 0.25
shot 01-the-ping-rings-out
wait 0.9
shot 02-the-name-on-its-own-glass
wait 4.0
shot 03-and-the-glass-is-taken-off-again

# --- A badge answers for the line the glass does not say -----------------------
# The burning presses with fumes and heat at once. Each one used to write a
# centred line of body text across the middle of the world, on top of the badge
# that was already saying it. Now the badge takes the line and brackets close on
# the tile, and the world keeps the space.
place burning
hour 19.5
await pressure_bites 20
await badge_answered 8
shot 04-the-badge-takes-the-line
# That the middle stays clear is not a second picture of the same thing: it is
# this await, which fails the tour if a line is ever written across the world
# while the burning presses.
await quiet_glass 8

# --- The survey, with every name knocked out of the coast ----------------------
# M opens it. A discovery's diamond used to be drawn after the lettering and
# landed on it: COAST read C-diamond-AST. Names are lettered last now, over
# nothing, on glass cleared outright.
place coast
tap map
await app:map 5
wait 0.6
shot 06-the-survey
# The survey's own scale key (E is `confirm` on any page, 90_ui PAGE_KEYS): one
# step in, so the names are lettered again at a scale the coast is denser at.
tap use
wait 0.5
shot 07-a-step-closer
tap map
await app:none 5

# --- What the machines left, named, and their survey through here --------------
# Home, then READS. The list said "cut in rows" four times with four different
# arrows; it names the work now, one row per kind, and the lower half of the
# pane carries their bearing with the works strung along it.
tap pause
choose reads
tap use
await app:reads 8
wait 0.7
shot 08-machine-reads
tap pause
tap pause
await app:none 5
shot 09-back-on-the-world
